I fixed #1185: when you run yoyo from your home directory, it no longer tells you your own config file is "not read". All checks pass (build, tests, clippy, fmt), and it's committed as 94dfdc2b.

The bug: In $HOME, ./.yoyo.toml and ~/.yoyo.toml are the same file, but existing_config_paths_in listed it twice. So yoyo config show printed the live config under "Also present, not read … nothing in the file(s) above takes effect". There was a second symptom I hadn't been asked about: /config set --global run from $HOME warned that the file it had just written was overridden by itself.

The fix (in src/config_paths.rs): existing_config_paths_in now passes through to a new existing_config_paths_with(project_dir, home, xdg). That function treats two entries as one when they resolve to the same file on disk, and keeps the higher-precedence one. Because every consumer reads this one function, they all get the fix together: /config show, the /config get note, the /config set shadow warning, and the setup wizard's shadow and demoted checks. The same check also covers a project or XDG config that is a symlink to ~/.yoyo.toml.

Tests:
• Same-file case: run from $HOME, tested both as the same path and through a symlinked directory. The file is listed once, and each consumer is checked on its own output.
• Different-files case: project, home and XDG files are all distinct. The home file is still reported as not read, so the genuine warning still fires.
• Proof the test catches the bug: I temporarily disabled the dedup, the first test failed by name, and I put the code back. No marker was left behind and all 37 tests in the module pass.

Built binary, using the repro from the issue:
• Before: it printed "Also present, not read: /tmp/…/.yoyo.toml".
• After: it prints only Loaded config: .yoyo.toml and model = m1.
• Run from a subdirectory with its own config: it still lists $HOME/.yoyo.toml as not read, which is correct.

No docs needed changing, since none of them describe the double listing. config_paths.rs is now 1875 lines, under the 2000-line cap.
